        "why_this_step": "This converts the diagnostic profile into a deployable mixed W/A PTQ assignment without changing the latent planner or replacing measured memory with a nominal byte assumption.",
        "linked_component": "both",
        "linked_falsification": "metric_specification",
        "input": "Site-by-bit influence profile, candidate bit pairs, backend-specific byte accounting, target bit histogram, and memory budget B.",
        "output": "A mixed W/A assignment `b*` and a quantized checkpoint candidate."
      },
      {
        "step_id": "S4",
        "title": "Preserve closed-loop action consequences",
        "what_changes": "Apply the assignment to the frozen planner and run its normal imagined action-sequence and receding-horizon loop, recording candidate-order agreement, first-action agreement, and closed-loop task success under the prescribed horizon conditions.",
        "why_this_step": "The object to preserve is the selected action and its later consequences, so the evaluation stays in latent-dynamics planning rather than switching to joint video/action denoising.",
        "linked_component": "engineering",
        "linked_falsification": "mechanism_distinguisher",
        "input": "Quantized checkpoint candidate and the unchanged planner and evaluation convention from S1.",
        "output": "Closed-loop rollout outcomes and decision-level diagnostics for assessing whether RankCal's allocation signal is load-bearing."
      }
    ],
    "design_reasoning_echo": "RankCal chooses the decision-level object because the failure of interest is an ordering change that propagates through receding-horizon action selection, not an isolated tensor distortion. Holding the planner, candidate pool, rollout convention, tie rule, and all non-target sites in FP16 makes the one-site intervention interpretable; aggregating its ranking influence into a memory-constrained assignment connects a diagnostic to deployment. The design therefore keeps the backbone and planner fixed, tests the allocation signal against local-reconstruction allocation, and stays separate from LeFlow's planner redesign and QuantWAMs' joint video/action denoising."
  }
}

